  "query_preview": "..\nWith the leading edge of the col now to the northwest of the islands,\nan area of slightly drier air and light to gentle northerlies has \nmoved in over the region. This pattern is conducive for island-effect\nshowers as air over the cooler ocean crosses the warmer islands from\nnorth to south, ending up as an unstable air mass by the time it\nreaches Guam. Wind speeds should gradually increase from Saipan to\nGuam, resulting in a trail of scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms over southern Guam and adjacent coastal waters. This \npattern is forecasted for Tuesday afternoon should the skies remain \nclear enough, followed by a slightly wetter period and isolated \nthunderstorms beginning Tuesday night through midweek, with winds \nshifting out of the northeast as a potential circulation approaches \nthe Marianas. In the latter half of the week, a dry easterly trade \npattern may return to the region, with isolated light showers (10% \nchance of showers) a possibility during the weekend.",
